//
// A TEMPLATE NAMING ITSELF IRREGULARLY IS REFUSED (`@FR-G-Regular`, crossed with `@FR-G-Type`).
// A template may name itself only at its own variables, UNCHANGED, and anything else is refused
// at the declaration: a variable wrapped in a former (`Bad<vector<T>>`, a struct, and the same
// for an enum `Lst<vector<T>>`), a tuple of the variable (`Wide<(T, T)>`), the variables
// SWAPPED (`Two<U, T>`), and a CONCRETE argument (`Fix<integer>`).  The last two do have a
// finite set of instances, so the diagnostic's "has no finite set of instances" over-states
// them — the rule's own test ("only at its own variables, unchanged") is what refuses them,
// and the claims pin the part every wording keeps.
//
// @falsified-at: none — pins a refusal the compiler already makes; the negation cell of a
//   rule that holds, not a fix, so no earlier build is a control for it.
// CHANNEL: expect — the five diagnostics.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: a build accepting a shape compiles the file and leaves its claim unanswered.
// HOLDS: the diagnostics are the same on both backends.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `Bad<vector<T>>` inside `Bad<T>`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `Lst<vector<T>>` inside `Lst<T>`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `Wide<(T, T)>` inside `Wide<T>`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `Two<U, T>` inside `Two<T, U>`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `Fix<integer>` inside `Fix<T>`
struct Bad<T> { v: T, n: vector<Bad<vector<T>>> }
enum Lst<T> { Nil, Cons { h: T, t: vector<Lst<vector<T>>> } }
struct Wide<T> { v: T, kids: vector<Wide<(T, T)>> }
struct Two<T, U> { a: T, b: U, kids: vector<Two<U, T>> }
struct Fix<T> { a: T, o: vector<Fix<integer>> }

fn main() { }
